---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, LINKS, NAME } from "../lib/site";

const link = "font-medium text-fg underline decoration-accent underline-offset-4";
---

<Base title={`Privacy · ${NAME}`} description="What the GrooveKit website and app do with your data.">
  <article class="container-site max-w-3xl py-24 sm:py-32">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="title mt-4">Your music stays on your Mac.</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-faint">Last updated 26 October 2026</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10 leading-relaxed text-dim [&_h2]:mb-3 [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:text-fg [&_li]:mt-2 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5">
      <section>
        <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
        <p>
          {CONTACT.name}, <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.email}`} class={link}>{CONTACT.email}</a>. GrooveKit is a free,
          non-commercial open-source project.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>This website</h2>
        <p>
          groovekit.org is a static site hosted on GitHub Pages. It sets no cookies, has no analytics or tracking, and
          loads nothing from other servers; even the font comes from this site. To deliver the pages and keep the
          service secure, GitHub, Inc. (USA) as the host receives your IP address and the usual request data. See the{" "}
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" class={link}>GitHub Privacy Statement</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Downloads, source code and issues</h2>
        <p>
          The app is downloaded from GitHub releases, and the source code and issues are on GitHub too, under
          GitHub’s terms. What you write in an issue is public.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The app</h2>
        <p>
          GrooveKit runs on your Mac. There’s no account, and it sends no analytics, telemetry or usage data
          anywhere, and doesn’t check for updates. Your songs, samples, recordings and settings stay on your Mac, and
          the engine accepts connections only from your own computer. Plugins you load are their makers’ software
          and follow their policies.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The agent</h2>
        <p>
          Only when you use the agent does data leave your Mac, and then it goes to the AI provider you chose, not to
          the GrooveKit project:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <span class="text-fg">The agent panel in the app</span> sends your messages, and what the agent reads of
            the song to carry them out (track and sample names, notes, mix settings, sample file paths, analysis
            results), to Anthropic’s API with your own API key, under Anthropic’s terms and{" "}
            <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy" class={link}>privacy policy</a>. Your key and the
            conversation are stored on your Mac. Voice messages are turned into text on your Mac, by Whisper; the
            recording isn’t sent anywhere, only the text, like a typed message. The speech model is downloaded once,
            from Hugging Face, when you first use the microphone and agree to it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="text-fg">Claude Code or another MCP client</span> sends the same kinds of data to whichever
            AI provider that client uses, under its terms.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          You can ask for access to, correction or deletion of your personal data, and complain to a data protection
          authority. The GrooveKit project itself collects no personal data, so there’s usually nothing to hand over;
          for the website’s server logs, GitHub is the one to ask. Questions go to{" "}
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.email}`} class={link}>{CONTACT.email}</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The source code of the website and the app is on{" "}
          <a href={LINKS.github} class={link}>GitHub</a>, so you can check all of this.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
